// Practice questions for the Digital Logic Design study page: one list per unit, each [marks, question, answer].
export const DLD_Q = [
  [
    [2, "Convert (156)₁₀ to binary.", "156 ÷ 2 = 78 R0; 78 ÷ 2 = 39 R0; 39 ÷ 2 = 19 R1; 19 ÷ 2 = 9 R1; 9 ÷ 2 = 4 R1; 4 ÷ 2 = 2 R0; 2 ÷ 2 = 1 R0; 1 ÷ 2 = 0 R1.\nRead the remainders upward: (156)₁₀ = (10011100)₂.\nCheck: 128 + 16 + 8 + 4 = 156 ✓"],
    [2, "What is the difference between a bit, a nibble and a byte?", "A bit is one binary digit (0 or 1). A nibble is 4 bits (one hex digit). A byte is 8 bits (two hex digits). These are the standard groupings for storing and sending binary data."],
    [2, "Find the 2’s complement of (−29) in 8 bits.", "+29 = 00011101. 1’s complement = 11100010. Add 1 → 11100011. This is −29 in 8-bit 2’s complement.\nCheck: 00011101 + 11100011 = 1 00000000; the carry is discarded, so the sum is 0 ✓"],
    [2, "State De Morgan’s theorems with examples.", "Theorem 1: (A·B)′ = A′ + B′ (NAND = OR of the complements).\nTheorem 2: (A + B)′ = A′·B′ (NOR = AND of the complements).\nTrick: break the bar at the operator, change AND ↔ OR, and put a bar on each variable."],
    [2, "Why are NAND and NOR called universal gates?", "A gate is universal if it alone can build NOT, AND and OR. Every Boolean function can be built from these three, so the gate can build any function.\nNAND: NOT = tie both inputs together; AND = NAND followed by NOT; OR = NAND with both inputs inverted. NOR works the same way."],
    [2, "Convert (3A7)₁₆ to binary and octal.", "Hex → binary: 3 = 0011, A = 1010, 7 = 0111 → (001110100111)₂.\nGroup in 3s from the right: 001 110 100 111 = 1 6 4 7 → (1647)₈."],
    [2, "What is Gray code and where is it used?", "Gray code is a binary sequence in which only one bit changes between successive values, for example 0000, 0001, 0011, 0010.\nIt is used in shaft encoders and on K-map axes to avoid false readings when several bits would change together."],
    [2, "Prove that A + A′B = A + B using Boolean algebra.", "A + A′B = (A + A′)(A + B)   [distributive law: X + YZ = (X + Y)(X + Z)]\n= 1·(A + B)   [complement law: A + A′ = 1]\n= A + B   [identity law]"],
    [2, "What is the range of a 6-bit 2’s complement number?", "Range = −2ⁿ⁻¹ to +(2ⁿ⁻¹ − 1). For n = 6: −32 to +31. The MSB has weight −32 and the other five bits give 0 to 31."],
    [2, "Should XOR or XNOR be used as a parity checker?", "XOR outputs 1 when an odd number of inputs are 1, so a chain of XOR gates over all data bits plus the parity bit checks even parity: 0 means no error, 1 means error. XNOR is used for the opposite (odd-parity) convention."],
    [3, "Explain with a diagram the step-by-step process of converting (45)₁₀ to binary.", "Method: repeated division by 2.\n1. Divide 45 by 2 and write the remainder: 45 ÷ 2 = 22 R1.\n2. Repeat with the quotient: 22 ÷ 2 = 11 R0; 11 ÷ 2 = 5 R1; 5 ÷ 2 = 2 R1; 2 ÷ 2 = 1 R0; 1 ÷ 2 = 0 R1.\n3. Stop when the quotient is 0.\n4. Read the remainders from bottom to top (LSB at the top, MSB at the bottom): (45)₁₀ = (101101)₂.\nCheck: 32 + 8 + 4 + 1 = 45 ✓\nDiagram: a column of divisions with the remainders listed on the right and an arrow pointing upward."],
    [3, "Draw the symbols and truth tables of AND, OR, NOT, NAND, NOR and XOR gates.", "For inputs AB = 00, 01, 10, 11:\nAND: 0 0 0 1\nOR: 0 1 1 1\nNAND: 1 1 1 0\nNOR: 1 0 0 0\nXOR: 0 1 1 0\nNOT: A = 0 → 1, A = 1 → 0.\nDraw the standard shape of each gate (AND: flat back and round front; OR: curved back; NOT: triangle with a bubble; NAND, NOR: AND, OR with a bubble; XOR: OR with an extra curve).\nKey facts: NAND and NOR are universal; XOR detects an odd number of 1s; XNOR detects equality."],
    [3, "Explain with a diagram how any gate can be implemented using only NAND gates.", "NOT: tie both inputs of a NAND together: (A·A)′ = A′.\nAND: NAND followed by a NOT (a second NAND with tied inputs): ((AB)′)′ = AB.\nOR: invert each input with a NAND, then NAND them: (A′·B′)′ = A + B (De Morgan).\nSince NOT, AND and OR can all be built, NAND alone can build any logic function, so NAND is a universal gate. NOR is universal by the same argument."],
    [3, "State and prove De Morgan’s theorems using truth tables.", "Theorem 1: (AB)′ = A′ + B′.   Theorem 2: (A + B)′ = A′B′.\nProof by perfect induction (AB = 00, 01, 10, 11):\n(AB)′ = 1, 1, 1, 0 and A′ + B′ = 1, 1, 1, 0 → equal.\n(A + B)′ = 1, 0, 0, 0 and A′B′ = 1, 0, 0, 0 → equal.\nSo the NAND of two signals equals the OR of their complements, and the NOR equals the AND of their complements."],
    [3, "Explain the 2’s complement subtraction method with a diagram. Perform 15 − 9 in 5 bits.", "Method: A − B = A + (2’s complement of B).\n1. +15 = 01111.\n2. +9 = 01001; 1’s complement = 10110; add 1 → 10111 (= −9).\n3. Add: 01111 + 10111 = 1 00110.\n4. Discard the final carry → 00110 = +6.\nSo 15 − 9 = 6 ✓\nRule: a final carry means the result is positive. No carry means the result is negative; take its 2’s complement to get the magnitude."],
    [3, "Simplify Y = A′B + AB′ + AB using Boolean algebra, then draw the circuit.", "1. Y = A′B + AB′ + AB\n2. = A′B + A(B′ + B)   [factor A]\n3. = A′B + A·1   [complement law: B′ + B = 1]\n4. = A′B + A   [identity]\n5. = A + B   [redundancy: A + A′B = A + B]\nThe original circuit needs 3 AND gates, 1 OR gate and 2 NOT gates (6 gates); the simplified Y = A + B needs one OR gate.\nTip: name each law you use, because examiners give marks per justified step."],
    [3, "Draw the circuit for Y = A′B + AB′ and show that it is the same as XOR.", "Circuit: 2 NOT gates (for A and B), 2 AND gates (A′B and AB′) and 1 OR gate = 5 gates.\nTruth table (AB = 00, 01, 10, 11): A′B = 0, 1, 0, 0; AB′ = 0, 0, 1, 0; Y = 0, 1, 1, 0, the same as A ⊕ B.\nA dedicated XOR gate (IC 7486) does this in one package. XOR is used in adders (sum = A ⊕ B), parity circuits and code converters."],
    [3, "Convert (2CA.8)₁₆ to binary and then to octal.", "Step 1: hex → binary, 4 bits per digit: 2 = 0010, C = 1100, A = 1010, .8 = .1000 → (001011001010.1000)₂.\nStep 2: group in 3s from the binary point.\nInteger: 001 011 001 010 → 1 3 1 2 → (1312)₈.\nFraction: 100 → 4 → (.4)₈.\nAnswer: (1312.4)₈.\nCheck: (2CA)₁₆ = 2×256 + 12×16 + 10 = 714 = 1×512 + 3×64 + 1×8 + 2 ✓"],
    [3, "List the Boolean identities and prove the absorption law A + AB = A using a truth table.", "Identities: A + 0 = A; A + 1 = 1; A·0 = 0; A·1 = A; A + A = A; A·A = A; A + A′ = 1; A·A′ = 0; (A′)′ = A; commutative, associative and distributive laws; De Morgan’s theorems.\nTruth table for A + AB (AB = 00, 01, 10, 11): AB = 0, 0, 0, 1; A + AB = 0, 0, 1, 1 = A ✓\nAlgebra: A + AB = A(1 + B) = A·1 = A.\nIntuition: if A = 1 the OR is already 1; if A = 0 then AB = 0 too. Either way the output equals A."],
    [7, "(a) Perform all four base conversions of (197.625)₁₀. (b) Verify each answer by converting back to decimal.", "Integer 197: 197 ÷ 2 → remainders 1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1 (read upward) = 11000101.\nFraction .625: ×2 = 1.25 → 1; .25 × 2 = 0.5 → 0; .5 × 2 = 1.0 → 1 → .101\n(197.625)₁₀ = (11000101.101)₂.\nOctal (groups of 3): 011 000 101 . 101 → (305.5)₈.\nHex (groups of 4): 1100 0101 . 1010 → (C5.A)₁₆.\nVerify:\nBinary: 128 + 64 + 4 + 1 + 0.5 + 0.125 = 197.625 ✓\nOctal: 3×64 + 0×8 + 5 + 5×0.125 = 197.625 ✓\nHex: 12×16 + 5 + 10×0.0625 = 197.625 ✓"],
    [7, "(a) Explain 2’s complement representation with a neat diagram. (b) Perform (i) 47 − 31 and (ii) 18 − 35 using 8-bit 2’s complement arithmetic. Detect overflow if any.", "(a) In n bits the MSB has weight −2ⁿ⁻¹ and all other bits have positive weights. To negate a number, invert all bits and add 1. Range: −2ⁿ⁻¹ to +2ⁿ⁻¹ − 1; for 8 bits −128 to +127. There is only one zero (00000000). Draw the 2’s complement number wheel: counting up goes round clockwise and the jump from +127 to −128 is the overflow boundary.\n(b)(i) 47 − 31: +47 = 00101111; +31 = 00011111; 2’s complement of 31 = 11100001.\n00101111 + 11100001 = 1 00010000. Discard the carry → 00010000 = +16 ✓. No overflow (adding numbers of opposite sign never overflows).\n(ii) 18 − 35: +18 = 00010010; 2’s complement of 35 (00100011) = 11011101.\n00010010 + 11011101 = 11101111. No carry, MSB = 1 → negative. 2’s complement of 11101111 = 00010001 = 17 → answer −17 ✓. No overflow."],
    [7, "(a) State and prove the single-variable Boolean theorems. (b) Simplify F = Σm(0, 2, 3, 5, 7) using a K-map. (c) Implement the simplified F with NAND gates only.", "(a) Proved by truth table (put A = 0 and A = 1): A + 0 = A, A + 1 = 1, A·0 = 0, A·1 = A, A + A = A, A·A = A, A + A′ = 1, A·A′ = 0, (A′)′ = A.\n(b) F = A′B′C′ + A′BC′ + A′BC + AB′C + ABC (m0, m2, m3, m5, m7). Groups of two on the K-map: {m0, m2} = A′C′; {m2, m3} = A′B; {m5, m7} = AC; {m3, m7} = BC.\nMinimal cover: F = A′C′ + A′B + AC (3 terms; m0 needs A′C′, m5 needs AC, m3 needs A′B or BC).\n(c) Double complement and De Morgan: F = ((A′C′)′·(A′B)′·(AC)′)′. Use three 2-input NAND gates for the product terms and one 3-input NAND gate for the output. The inverters for A and C are NAND gates with tied inputs. Total = 2 + 3 + 1 = 6 NAND gates."],
    [7, "(a) Explain all the Boolean operators with symbols, truth tables and IC numbers. (b) Show that XOR and XNOR are complements. (c) Express XOR using only NAND gates.", "(a) NOT 7404, AND 7408, OR 7432, NAND 7400, NOR 7402, XOR 7486, XNOR 74266 (or 7486 with inverter). Truth tables for AB = 00, 01, 10, 11: AND 0001, OR 0111, NAND 1110, NOR 1000, XOR 0110, XNOR 1001; NOT: 0 → 1, 1 → 0.\n(b) Truth table: XOR = 0, 1, 1, 0 and XNOR = 1, 0, 0, 1. XOR + XNOR = 1 in every row and XOR·XNOR = 0 in every row, so XNOR = (A ⊕ B)′, i.e. A ⊙ B = (A ⊕ B)′ ✓.\n(c) A ⊕ B from four NAND gates: G1 = (AB)′; G2 = (A·G1)′; G3 = (B·G1)′; G4 = (G2·G3)′ = A ⊕ B."],
    [7, "Explain the alternate logic-gate representations using De Morgan’s theorem.", "Every gate has a second, equivalent symbol: change the body (AND ↔ OR) and invert every terminal (add or remove a bubble).\nAND = OR with inverted inputs and output (so a NAND is an OR with inverted inputs).\nOR = AND with inverted inputs and output (so a NOR is an AND with inverted inputs).\nNAND: output is 0 only when all inputs are 1; alternate symbol: OR with inverted inputs, output is 1 when any input is 0 (active-LOW inputs).\nNOR: output 1 only when all inputs are 0; alternate symbol: AND with inverted inputs.\nNOT: a buffer with an inverted output.\nUse: choose the symbol that matches the active level of the signal, so that bubbles join bubbles and the circuit reads easily."],
  ],
  [
    [2, "What is a combinational circuit?", "A circuit whose outputs depend only on the present inputs. It has no memory and no feedback. Examples: adders, multiplexers, decoders, comparators."],
    [2, "Define minterm and maxterm.", "A minterm is an AND term containing every variable once (complemented or not); it is 1 for exactly one input row. A maxterm is an OR term containing every variable once; it is 0 for exactly one row. mᵢ and Mᵢ are complements."],
    [2, "Why is Gray code order used on K-map axes?", "Adjacent Gray codes differ in only one bit, so neighbouring cells differ in one variable. Grouping them removes that variable, because X + X′ = 1."],
    [2, "What is a don’t-care condition?", "An input combination that never occurs or whose output does not matter (for example codes 10–15 in BCD). It is marked X and can be taken as 1 or 0, whichever gives larger groups."],
    [2, "Define fan-out.", "The maximum number of standard gate inputs that one gate output can drive without going outside its voltage and current limits. Standard TTL has a fan-out of 10."],
    [2, "Write the sum and carry expressions of a full adder.", "S = A ⊕ B ⊕ Cin\nCout = AB + BCin + ACin = AB + Cin(A ⊕ B)"],
    [2, "What is a static-1 hazard?", "A momentary 1 → 0 → 1 glitch at the output when the output should stay at 1, caused by unequal path delays when one input changes."],
    [2, "Why is 0110 added in a BCD adder?", "BCD uses only 10 of the 16 four-bit codes. Adding 6 skips the six invalid codes (1010–1111), so a sum above 9 becomes a correct BCD digit with a decimal carry."],
    [3, "Express F = Σm(1, 4, 5, 6, 7) in SOP and POS form.", "Rows with F = 1 give the SOP: F = A′B′C + AB′C′ + AB′C + ABC′ + ABC.\nRows with F = 0 (m0, m2, m3) give the POS: F = ΠM(0, 2, 3) = (A + B + C)(A + B′ + C)(A + B′ + C′).\nMinimised with a K-map: F = A + B′C."],
    [3, "Explain why adjacent cells of a K-map can be combined.", "Adjacent cells differ in exactly one variable, because the axes use Gray code. When two such minterms are OR-ed, that variable appears in both complemented and uncomplemented form and disappears: X·Y + X′·Y = Y. Groups of 2, 4, 8 … cells remove 1, 2, 3 … variables. The map edges also count as adjacent."],
    [3, "Simplify F = Σm(1, 3, 7, 11, 15) + d(0, 2, 5) using a K-map.", "Variables A B C D. Quad {m3, m7, m11, m15} = CD.\nThe don’t-cares at 0 and 2 allow the quad {m0, m1, m2, m3} = A′B′.\nm5 is not needed, so it is left as 0.\nF = A′B′ + CD."],
    [3, "Implement F = AB + CD using NAND gates only.", "AND–OR and NAND–NAND forms are identical.\nF = AB + CD = ((AB)′·(CD)′)′.\nUse two 2-input NAND gates for AB and CD and a third 2-input NAND gate whose inputs are their outputs. Total = 3 NAND gates. The two bubbles on each internal wire cancel, so the circuit works as AND–OR."],
    [3, "What is a static hazard? Explain with an example and remove it.", "A static hazard is a glitch on an output that should stay constant when one input changes. It happens when the output depends on two paths with different delays.\nExample: F = AB + B′C with A = C = 1. When B falls from 1 to 0, AB turns off before B′C turns on, so F briefly drops to 0 (static-1 hazard).\nFix: add the consensus term AC: F = AB + B′C + AC. With A = C = 1 the term AC holds F at 1 during the change."],
    [3, "Find the noise margins if VOH = 2.7 V, VOL = 0.5 V, VIH = 2.0 V, VIL = 0.8 V.", "High-state noise margin NMH = VOH − VIH = 2.7 − 2.0 = 0.7 V.\nLow-state noise margin NML = VIL − VOL = 0.8 − 0.5 = 0.3 V.\nThe circuit tolerates a noise of at least 0.3 V (the smaller margin)."],
    [3, "Calculate the fan-out of a 74LS gate: IOH = 0.4 mA, IIH = 20 µA, IOL = 8 mA, IIL = 0.4 mA.", "HIGH state: IOH / IIH = 0.4 mA / 20 µA = 20.\nLOW state: IOL / IIL = 8 mA / 0.4 mA = 20.\nFan-out = the smaller of the two = 20."],
    [3, "Design a half adder.", "Inputs A, B; outputs Sum S and Carry C.\nTruth table (AB = 00, 01, 10, 11): S = 0, 1, 1, 0; C = 0, 0, 0, 1.\nS = A ⊕ B (1 when the inputs differ); C = AB.\nCircuit: one XOR gate and one AND gate. It has no carry-in, so it is used only for the LSB or inside a full adder."],
    [3, "Convert binary 1011 to Gray code and draw the converter.", "Rule: G(n−1) = B(n−1); Gᵢ = Bᵢ₊₁ ⊕ Bᵢ.\nB3B2B1B0 = 1011 → G3 = 1; G2 = 1 ⊕ 0 = 1; G1 = 0 ⊕ 1 = 1; G0 = 1 ⊕ 1 = 0 → Gray = 1110.\nCircuit: G3 = B3 and three XOR gates, each taking two adjacent binary bits.\n(Gray → binary: B3 = G3, Bᵢ = Bᵢ₊₁ ⊕ Gᵢ, so each XOR uses the previous output.)"],
    [3, "Design a 3-bit even-parity generator.", "Parity bit P makes the total number of 1s even: P = 1 when the number of 1s among A, B, C is odd.\nP = A ⊕ B ⊕ C (two XOR gates).\nChecker (4-bit, with P received): E = A ⊕ B ⊕ C ⊕ P; E = 0 means no error, E = 1 means error."],
    [3, "Design a 1-bit magnitude comparator.", "Outputs: G (A > B), E (A = B), L (A < B).\nG = AB′; E = (A ⊕ B)′ (XNOR); L = A′B.\nTruth table (AB = 00, 01, 10, 11): G = 0, 0, 1, 0; E = 1, 0, 0, 1; L = 0, 1, 0, 0. Exactly one output is 1 at any time."],
    [3, "Explain propagation delay with a waveform.", "Delays are measured between the 50 % points of the input and output waveforms.\ntPHL: delay when the output goes HIGH → LOW.\ntPLH: delay when the output goes LOW → HIGH.\nAverage propagation delay: tpd = (tPHL + tPLH) / 2.\nThe delay of a circuit is the sum of the gate delays along its longest (critical) path."],
    [7, "Minimise F(A, B, C, D) = Σm(0, 1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 12, 13, 14) using a K-map and implement it using NAND gates only.", "Step 1: the zeros are m3, m7, m10, m11, m15, so the map has 11 ones.\nStep 2: groups. Octet {0, 1, 4, 5, 8, 9, 12, 13} = C′. The remaining ones are m2, m6 and m14. Quad {0, 2, 4, 6} = A′D′ and quad {4, 6, 12, 14} = BD′ cover them (overlapping with the octet is allowed).\nF = C′ + A′D′ + BD′.\nStep 3 (NAND): F = (C·(A′D′)′·(BD′)′)′.\nThe inverters for A and D are tied-input NANDs (2 gates). Then NAND(A′, D′), NAND(B, D′) and a 3-input NAND with inputs C, and these two outputs.\nTotal = 2 + 2 + 1 = 5 NAND gates."],
    [7, "Obtain the minimal SOP and POS expressions of F = Σm(0, 1, 2, 5, 8, 9, 10) using K-maps and compare them.", "SOP (group the ones): quad {0, 2, 8, 10} = B′D′; quad {0, 1, 8, 9} = B′C′; pair {1, 5} = A′C′D.\nF = B′D′ + B′C′ + A′C′D (7 literals).\nPOS (group the zeros, m3, m4, m6, m7, m11, m12, m13, m14, m15): quad {3, 7, 11, 15} = CD; quad {4, 6, 12, 14} = BD′; quad {12, 13, 14, 15} = AB.\nF′ = CD + BD′ + AB, so F = (C′ + D′)(B′ + D)(A′ + B′) (6 literals).\nComparison: the POS needs fewer literals (6 against 7), so it is cheaper here. Both give the same truth table."],
    [7, "Design a full adder using two half adders. Using it, construct a 4-bit ripple-carry adder and find its worst-case delay.", "Full adder: inputs A, B, Cin; outputs S, Cout.\nHA1 adds A and B: S1 = A ⊕ B, C1 = AB.\nHA2 adds S1 and Cin: S = S1 ⊕ Cin, C2 = S1·Cin.\nCout = C1 + C2 (an OR gate) = AB + Cin(A ⊕ B).\n4-bit ripple-carry adder: connect four full adders; Cout of each stage goes to Cin of the next; the first Cin = 0. Inputs A3…A0, B3…B0; outputs S3…S0 and the final carry C4.\nDelay: each stage passes the carry through an AND and an OR, 2 gate delays. The first carry takes the XOR as well, so the worst case for n bits is about 2n + 1 gate delays: for n = 4, 9 gate delays (with the XOR for subtraction). If one full-adder carry takes t, the delay is about n·t. This linear growth is why large adders use carry look-ahead."],
    [7, "Design a single-digit BCD adder. Explain its working with the examples 7 + 6 and 9 + 8.", "Step 1: add the two BCD digits with a 4-bit binary adder (Cin = carry from the previous digit): Z = A + B + Cin, giving carry K1 and sum bits Z3 Z2 Z1 Z0.\nStep 2: the result is wrong when it is above 9. Correction needed when K = K1 + Z3Z2 + Z3Z1 = 1.\nStep 3: if K = 1 add 0110 using a second 4-bit adder (K is also the BCD carry out); if K = 0 add 0000.\nExample 7 + 6: 0111 + 0110 = 1101 (13, invalid; Z3Z2 = 1) → K = 1 → 1101 + 0110 = 1 0011 → output digit 0011 (3) with carry 1: BCD 13 ✓.\nExample 9 + 8: 1001 + 1000 = 1 0001 (K1 = 1) → K = 1 → 0001 + 0110 = 0111 (7) with carry 1: BCD 17 ✓."],
    [7, "Design a 4-bit binary adder–subtractor using full adders and XOR gates. Explain overflow detection with examples.", "Circuit: four full adders in ripple form. Each B input passes through an XOR gate with a control input M. The first carry-in is M.\nM = 0: B ⊕ 0 = B and Cin = 0 → S = A + B.\nM = 1: B ⊕ 1 = B′ and Cin = 1 → S = A + B′ + 1 = A − B (2’s complement subtraction).\nOverflow (signed numbers) happens when the sign of the result is wrong: V = C4 ⊕ C3 (carry into the MSB XOR carry out of the MSB). Equivalent: two numbers of the same sign give a result of the opposite sign.\nExample 1: 0101 + 0011 (5 + 3): sum 1000 = −8; C3 = 1, C4 = 0 → V = 1, overflow (the true result 8 does not fit 4 bits).\nExample 2: 0011 + 0010 (3 + 2): sum 0101; C3 = 0, C4 = 0 → V = 0, no overflow.\nExample 3 (subtraction, M = 1): 7 − (−2) with A = 0111, B = 1110: 0111 + 0001 + 1 = 1001 = −7 instead of +9; C3 = 1, C4 = 0 → V = 1, overflow."],
    [7, "Design a BCD to Excess-3 code converter. Show the truth table, all K-maps and the circuit.", "Excess-3 = BCD + 3. Inputs A B C D (A is the MSB), outputs W X Y Z. Codes 10–15 are don’t-cares.\nTruth table (BCD → Excess-3): 0000→0011, 0001→0100, 0010→0101, 0011→0110, 0100→0111, 0101→1000, 0110→1001, 0111→1010, 1000→1011, 1001→1100.\nK-map simplification (with don’t-cares):\nW = A + BC + BD\nX = B′C + B′D + BC′D′\nY = CD + C′D′\nZ = D′\nCircuit: one inverter for Z; XNOR-like logic for Y (CD + C′D′); AND–OR networks for W and X.\nCheck 0101 (5): W = 0 + 0 + 1·1 = 1; X = 0 + 0 + 0 = 0; Y = 0 + 0 = 0; Z = 0 → 1000 (8) ✓."],
  ],
  [
    [3, "Differentiate between combinational and sequential circuits.", "Combinational: output depends on the present inputs only; no memory; no feedback; usually no clock. Examples: adder, multiplexer, decoder.\nSequential: output depends on the present inputs and the past state; has memory (flip-flops) and feedback; usually clocked. Examples: counter, register, RAM.\nBlock diagram: a sequential circuit is a combinational block plus a memory block whose present state is fed back as extra input, with next state fed to the memory on the clock."],
    [3, "What is a bistable element? How does it store one bit?", "Two inverters connected in a ring. If Q = 1, the second inverter gives Q′ = 0, which the first inverter turns back into Q = 1: the state reinforces itself. Q = 0 is equally stable. Two stable states mean one stored bit.\nLimitation: it has no input to change the stored bit, so NOR or NAND gates are used instead to get set and reset inputs (the latch)."],
    [3, "Explain the S-R latch using NOR gates with its function table.", "Two cross-coupled NOR gates: Q = NOR(R, Q′) and Q′ = NOR(S, Q).\nFunction table (S R → Q⁺): 0 0 → Q (hold); 1 0 → 1 (set); 0 1 → 0 (reset); 1 1 → invalid (Q = Q′ = 0).\nNOR gives 0 if any input is 1. S = 1 forces Q′ = 0 and so Q = 1; R = 1 forces Q = 0. With S = R = 0 the latch keeps its state. With S = R = 1 both outputs are 0 and, if both inputs then return to 0 together, the final state is unpredictable, so S = R = 1 is not allowed."],
    [3, "Explain the S′-R′ latch using NAND gates.", "Two cross-coupled NAND gates: Q = NAND(S′, Q′), Q′ = NAND(R′, Q).\nFunction table (S′ R′ → Q⁺): 1 1 → Q (hold); 0 1 → 1 (set); 1 0 → 0 (reset); 0 0 → invalid (Q = Q′ = 1).\nNAND gives 1 if any input is 0, so the inputs are active-LOW: a LOW on S′ sets Q = 1; a LOW on R′ resets Q = 0; both HIGH is hold; both LOW is invalid."],
    [3, "Draw a gated D latch and explain why it has no invalid state.", "A gated S-R latch (two steering NAND gates passing S and R only while EN = 1) with S = D and R = D′, using one inverter.\nBecause R is always the complement of S, S and R can never both be 1, so the invalid state cannot occur.\nEN = 0: the latch holds. EN = 1: Q follows D (Q = D, Q′ = D′). It is transparent while EN = 1 and latched when EN = 0."],
    [3, "Distinguish between a latch and a flip-flop with a timing diagram.", "Latch: level-sensitive; its output follows the input during the whole time the enable (clock) is HIGH; the output can change many times in one pulse.\nFlip-flop: edge-triggered; the output changes only at the active clock edge (rising or falling) and stays constant in between.\nTiming diagram: for the same D and CLK, the latch output Q follows D during CLK = 1, while the flip-flop output changes only at each rising edge, taking the value of D at that instant."],
    [3, "How is edge triggering achieved? Explain the pulse-transition detector.", "A pulse-transition detector makes a very narrow pulse at each active clock edge. It uses an inverter (delay) and an AND gate: CLK · CLK′(delayed). The AND output is 1 only for a short time Δt (the delay of the inverter) after each rising edge.\nThis narrow pulse is used as the enable of a latch, so the latch is open only for a few nanoseconds after the rising edge and D is sampled at that instant. A falling-edge version uses a NOR/OR arrangement with the inverted clock."],
    [3, "Explain the asynchronous inputs PRESET and CLEAR of a flip-flop.", "PRESET (PRE′) and CLEAR (CLR′) are active-LOW inputs that act at once, without the clock.\nPRE′ = 0 sets Q = 1; CLR′ = 0 resets Q = 0. Both are kept at 1 during normal clocked operation.\nBoth LOW together is not allowed (invalid state). They are used to start a counter or register in a known state, for example at power-up."],
    [3, "Derive the characteristic equation of a J-K flip-flop.", "Characteristic table: Q⁺ = 1 for (J K Q) = 0 0 1, 1 0 0, 1 0 1 and 1 1 0 (minterms 1, 4, 5, 6 of J K Q).\nK-map: pair {m4, m6} (J = 1, Q = 0) = JQ′; pair {m1, m5} (K = 0, Q = 1) = K′Q.\nQ⁺ = JQ′ + K′Q."],
    [3, "Write the excitation table of a J-K flip-flop and explain it.", "Q → Q⁺ : J K\n0 → 0 : 0 X (hold or reset both keep 0)\n0 → 1 : 1 X (set or toggle both give 1)\n1 → 0 : X 1 (reset or toggle both give 0)\n1 → 1 : X 0 (hold or set both keep 1)\nThe table is read backwards from the characteristic table: it tells which inputs are needed to get a wanted change of state. X means don’t care."],
    [3, "Explain how a T flip-flop acts as a frequency divider.", "With T = 1 the output toggles on every rising clock edge, so one full output period takes two clock periods.\nf(Q) = f(CLK) / 2.\nCascading n T flip-flops (each clocked by the previous output) divides the frequency by 2ⁿ. Example: 3 flip-flops divide 16 kHz to 2 kHz."],
    [3, "Convert a J-K flip-flop into a T flip-flop and a D flip-flop into a T flip-flop.", "J-K → T: join J and K and call the common input T. T = 0 → J = K = 0 (hold); T = 1 → J = K = 1 (toggle). Q⁺ = TQ′ + T′Q = T ⊕ Q.\nD → T: the D flip-flop needs D = Q⁺ = T ⊕ Q. So connect an XOR gate: inputs T and Q (fed back), output to D."],
    [7, "Explain the S-R latch using NOR gates and the S′-R′ latch using NAND gates with circuits, function tables and timing diagrams.", "NOR latch: Q = (R + Q′)′, Q′ = (S + Q)′. Table (S R → Q⁺): 00 hold, 10 set, 01 reset, 11 invalid. It reacts to HIGH pulses.\nNAND latch: Q = (S′·Q′)′, Q′ = (R′·Q)′. Table (S′ R′ → Q⁺): 11 hold, 01 set, 10 reset, 00 invalid. It reacts to LOW pulses (active-LOW inputs).\nTiming diagram: a pulse on S sets Q = 1 and Q stays 1 after the pulse ends; a pulse on R resets Q = 0; the NAND version behaves the same with LOW pulses.\nInvalid state: both outputs are equal (0 for NOR, 1 for NAND), so Q and Q′ are no longer complements, and if both inputs return to the rest level together the gates race and the final state is random. Designers therefore guarantee S·R = 0 (NOR) or S′ + R′ = 1 (NAND).\nSummary: NOR latch 00 hold, 10 set, 01 reset, 11 invalid; NAND latch 11 hold, 01 set, 10 reset, 00 invalid."],
    [7, "Explain the gated D latch. How is it converted to an edge-triggered D flip-flop? Explain the D flip-flop with asynchronous preset and clear inputs.", "Step 1: start from a gated S-R latch. Two steering NAND gates pass S and R only when EN = 1. EN = 0 holds the state; EN = 1 lets the latch follow S and R, but S = R = 1 is still possible.\nStep 2: connect S = D and R = D′ with an inverter, so S and R are never equal. The invalid state is gone: this is the gated D latch.\nStep 3: it is transparent while EN = 1 (Q follows D) and latched when EN = 0. Problem: while EN = 1 every change of D reaches Q (level triggering).\nStep 4: to get an edge-triggered D flip-flop, drive EN from a pulse-transition detector (CLK · CLK′ delayed). The latch is enabled only for a few ns after each rising edge, so D is sampled once per clock.\nStep 5: comparison: with the same inputs the latch output follows D for the whole high period, while the flip-flop output changes only at the edge. Q⁺ = D at the edge.\nStep 6: asynchronous inputs: PRE′ = 0 sets Q = 1 and CLR′ = 0 resets Q = 0 immediately, overriding the clock.\nFinal: D latch: Q follows D while EN = 1. Edge-triggered D flip-flop: Q⁺ = D only at the clock edge. PRE and CLR force Q = 1 and Q = 0 without the clock."],
    [7, "What is the race-around condition in a J-K flip-flop? Explain how it is eliminated using a master-slave J-K flip-flop with circuit and timing diagram.", "Step 1: inside a J-K flip-flop S = JQ′ and R = KQ, so the outputs are fed back to the input gates. This feedback makes J = K = 1 toggle instead of being invalid.\nStep 2: in a level-triggered J-K with J = K = 1 and CLK = 1, Q toggles, the new Q is fed back, and Q toggles again after tpd. If the pulse width tp > tpd, Q oscillates and its final value is unknown. This is the race-around condition.\nStep 3: it is avoided if tp < tpd < T, but tpd is only a few ns, so this is hard.\nStep 4: remedies: (1) a narrow clock pulse (impractical); (2) edge triggering; (3) master-slave.\nStep 5: master-slave J-K: two J-K latches in series, the master enabled when CLK = 1 and the slave when CLK = 0, with the feedback taken from the slave outputs. (i) CLK = 1: the master follows J and K while the slave is disabled, so Q (feedback) is frozen and the master changes at most once. (ii) CLK falls 1 → 0: the master is disabled and the slave copies the master to Q. (iii) CLK = 0: the master ignores J and K.\nStep 6: timing: the master changes once while CLK = 1 and the slave copies on the falling edge, giving one toggle per clock with J = K = 1.\nFinal: race-around is repeated toggling of a level-triggered J-K when J = K = 1 and tp > tpd. It is eliminated by edge triggering or the master-slave arrangement, because the feedback loop is always broken."],
    [7, "Derive the characteristic equations and excitation tables of S-R, J-K, D and T flip-flops.", "Method: (1) write the characteristic table (Q and inputs → Q⁺); (2) K-map of Q⁺; (3) read the characteristic equation; (4) read the table backwards for the excitation table.\nCharacteristic equations:\nSR: Q⁺ = S + R′Q (with SR = 0)\nJK: Q⁺ = JQ′ + K′Q\nD: Q⁺ = D\nT: Q⁺ = T ⊕ Q = TQ′ + T′Q\nExcitation tables (Q → Q⁺ : inputs):\n0 → 0 : SR = 0X, JK = 0X, D = 0, T = 0\n0 → 1 : SR = 10, JK = 1X, D = 1, T = 1\n1 → 0 : SR = 01, JK = X1, D = 0, T = 1\n1 → 1 : SR = X0, JK = X0, D = 1, T = 0\nExample (SR, 1 → 1): the latch can hold (S = 0, R = 0) or be set again (S = 1, R = 0), so R must be 0 and S is a don’t care."],
    [7, "Convert an S-R flip-flop into a J-K flip-flop. Show the conversion table, K-maps and circuit.", "Procedure: (1) write the characteristic table of the required flip-flop (J-K); (2) for each row find the S and R needed using the S-R excitation table; (3) minimise S and R as functions of J, K and Q; (4) draw and verify.\nS-R excitation table: 0→0: S R = 0 X; 0→1: 1 0; 1→0: 0 1; 1→1: X 0.\nConversion table (J K Q → Q⁺ : S R): 000→0: 0X; 001→1: X0; 010→0: 0X; 011→0: 01; 100→1: 10; 101→1: X0; 110→1: 10; 111→0: 01.\nK-maps give S = JQ′ and R = KQ.\nCircuit: two AND gates in front of the S-R flip-flop (J·Q′ → S and K·Q → R), with Q and Q′ fed back.\nCheck: J = K = 1, Q = 0 → S = 1, R = 0 → set → Q = 1 ✓. J = K = 1, Q = 1 → S = 0, R = 1 → reset → Q = 0 ✓. S and R are never both 1 because one needs Q and the other Q′.\nThe same procedure converts any flip-flop into any other."],
    [7, "Define set-up time, hold time and propagation delay of a positive-edge D flip-flop. If tpd = 10 ns, tcomb = 15 ns (min 3 ns), tsu = 5 ns and th = 2 ns, find the maximum clock frequency and check a 50 MHz clock.", "Definitions: set-up time tsu = minimum time D must be stable before the clock edge; hold time th = minimum time D must stay stable after the edge; propagation delay tpd = time from the clock edge to the change of Q.\nIf D changes inside the window tsu + th the flip-flop may become metastable and store a random value, so every data path must deliver its new value at least tsu before the next edge.\nPath: FF1 → combinational logic → FF2 with the same clock.\nTmin = tpd + tcomb(max) + tsu = 10 + 15 + 5 = 30 ns.\nfmax = 1 / 30 ns ≈ 33.3 MHz.\nCheck 50 MHz: T = 20 ns < 30 ns, so the data arrives 10 ns too late (slack = −10 ns). Set-up violation: the circuit fails at 50 MHz.\nHold check: earliest new data at FF2 = tpd + tcomb(min) = 10 + 3 = 13 ns after the edge, which is greater than th = 2 ns, so hold time is satisfied.\nFinal: Tmin = 30 ns, fmax ≈ 33.3 MHz; 50 MHz violates set-up; hold is satisfied."],
  ],
  [
    [3, "What is a MOD-N counter? How many flip-flops are needed for MOD-10 and MOD-60 counters?", "A MOD-N counter has N distinct states and returns to its starting state after N clock pulses. It needs the smallest n with 2ⁿ ≥ N.\nMOD-10: 2³ = 8 < 10 ≤ 2⁴ = 16 → 4 flip-flops.\nMOD-60: 2⁵ = 32 < 60 ≤ 2⁶ = 64 → 6 flip-flops (or a MOD-10 and a MOD-6 counter in cascade).\nThe state diagram of a MOD-8 counter is a ring of 8 states."],
    [3, "Explain frequency division using flip-flops. Find the output frequency of a 3-stage divider driven by a 1 MHz clock.", "A flip-flop with J = K = 1 (or T = 1) toggles once per clock, so Q completes one cycle every two clock cycles: f(Q) = f/2. Each toggling stage halves the frequency.\nThree stages: 1 MHz → 500 kHz → 250 kHz → 125 kHz = f / 2³.\nIn general n stages divide by 2ⁿ."],
    [3, "Draw a 3-bit asynchronous up counter and its timing diagram.", "Use three negative-edge J-K flip-flops with J = K = 1. The clock goes only to FF0; Q0 clocks FF1; Q1 clocks FF2.\nFF0 toggles on each falling edge of CLK, FF1 on each falling edge of Q0, FF2 on each falling edge of Q1.\nCount sequence (Q2 Q1 Q0): 000, 001, 010, 011, 100, 101, 110, 111, 000 …\nTiming diagram: Q0 has half the clock frequency, Q1 a quarter and Q2 an eighth."],
    [3, "How is an asynchronous counter made to count down?", "A stage must toggle when the previous stage goes from 0 to 1 (a borrow). With negative-edge flip-flops this is a falling edge of the complement, so clock each stage from the previous Q′ output. With positive-edge flip-flops, clock each stage from the previous Q output.\nCount sequence of a 3-bit down counter: 000, 111, 110, 101, 100, 011, 010, 001, 000 …"],
    [3, "Explain the effect of propagation delay in a ripple counter. Find fmax for a 4-bit counter with tpd = 10 ns.", "Each stage waits for the previous one, so the delays add up and temporary wrong (false) states appear. Example: 111 → 000 passes through the false states 110 (6) and 100 (4) before settling at 0.\nThe clock period must be at least N·tpd, so fmax = 1 / (N·tpd) = 1 / (4 × 10 ns) = 25 MHz."],
    [3, "Design a MOD-10 ripple counter.", "Use 4 flip-flops (2⁴ ≥ 10) with asynchronous clear, connected as a 4-bit ripple counter.\n10 = 1010, so Q3 = Q1 = 1 is the first count in which both are HIGH. Connect NAND(Q3, Q1) to the CLR′ inputs.\nWhen the count reaches 1010 the NAND output goes LOW and clears the counter to 0000. The counter shows 0–9; state 10 lasts only a few ns."],
    [3, "Compare asynchronous and synchronous counters.", "Asynchronous (ripple): the clock is applied only to the first flip-flop; each later stage is clocked by the previous one; the delay grows with N (N·tpd); transient false states and glitches occur; simple hardware; low speed.\nSynchronous: all flip-flops share the same clock; they change together; delay = tpd + tgate (fast), independent of N; no false states; needs extra gates; high speed.\nExample: 4-bit counter with tpd = 10 ns, tAND = 5 ns, tsu = 5 ns: synchronous clock period = 10 + 5 + 5 = 20 ns (50 MHz); a ripple counter needs about 4 × 10 + 5 = 45 ns."],
    [3, "Draw a 3-bit synchronous up counter and explain its logic.", "Three J-K (or T) flip-flops clocked by the same clock with J0 = K0 = 1, J1 = K1 = Q0, J2 = K2 = Q0·Q1.\nIn binary counting a bit toggles only when all lower bits are 1, so FF1 toggles when Q0 = 1 and FF2 toggles when Q0 = Q1 = 1. All flip-flops change together on the same clock edge, so there are no ripple delays or false states."],
    [3, "Explain a 4-bit ring counter with its state table.", "A shift register of four D flip-flops with Q3 fed back to D0. It must be preset to 1000 (preset FF0, clear the others); the single 1 circulates.\nClock: Q0Q1Q2Q3 = 0: 1000; 1: 0100; 2: 0010; 3: 0001; 4: 1000.\n4 flip-flops give 4 states, and every state has one HIGH output, so no decoder is needed. If it starts at 0000 it stays at 0000."],
    [3, "Explain a 4-bit Johnson counter. How many states does it have?", "A shift register in which the complement of the last output is fed back to the first input (Q3′ → D0), starting at 0000.\nSequence (Q0Q1Q2Q3): 0000, 1000, 1100, 1110, 1111, 0111, 0011, 0001, back to 0000.\nN flip-flops give 2N = 8 states. Each state is decoded by one 2-input AND gate, and each output is a square wave shifted by one clock."],
    [3, "Differentiate Moore and Mealy machines.", "Moore: output depends on the present state only; output written inside the state circle; output changes only at clock edges.\nMealy: output depends on the present state and the present inputs; output written on the arrow (input/output); output can change whenever the input changes.\nBlock diagram: in a Mealy machine the inputs also go to the output logic."],
    [3, "Explain a 4-bit SIPO shift register with a data-movement table.", "Four D flip-flops with each Q feeding the next D, and all four Q outputs available.\nSerial input 1, 1, 0, 1 (Q0 Q1 Q2 Q3): initial 0000; clock 1: 1000; clock 2: 1100; clock 3: 0110; clock 4: 1011.\nOne bit enters per clock and every stored bit moves one place right. After 4 clocks the whole word is available on Q0–Q3 at the same time: serial-to-parallel conversion."],
    [7, "Design a 3-bit asynchronous up counter using J-K flip-flops. Draw the timing diagram, explain the effect of propagation delay and show how it is converted to a MOD-6 counter.", "Step 1: three negative-edge J-K flip-flops with J = K = 1. CLK drives FF0; Q0 drives the clock of FF1; Q1 drives the clock of FF2.\nStep 2: sequence Q2Q1Q0: 000, 001, 010, 011, 100, 101, 110, 111, 000. The timing diagram shows Q0 = f/2, Q1 = f/4 and Q2 = f/8.\nStep 3: propagation delay: each stage responds tpd after the previous one, so the total delay is 3·tpd. During 111 → 000 the counter passes through false states 110 and 100. fmax = 1 / (3·tpd) (plus decoding delay).\nStep 4: MOD-6: the first unwanted count is 110, where Q2 = Q1 = 1. Connect NAND(Q2, Q1) to the CLR′ inputs of all flip-flops. At 110 the counter clears to 000 at once, so the counter shows 0–5 (state 6 lasts only a few ns).\nFinal: a 3-bit ripple counter divides by 8; with NAND(Q2, Q1) feeding CLR′ it divides by 6."],
    [7, "Design a synchronous MOD-6 counter using J-K flip-flops. Show the state diagram, state table, K-maps, circuit and lock-out check.", "Step 1: 3 flip-flops (2³ ≥ 6). State diagram: 000 → 001 → 010 → 011 → 100 → 101 → 000. States 110 and 111 are unused (don’t-cares).\nStep 2: JK excitation table: 0→0: 0X; 0→1: 1X; 1→0: X1; 1→1: X0. Fill J and K for each present state.\nStep 3: K-maps with X in 110 and 111:\nJ0 = K0 = 1\nJ1 = Q2′Q0, K1 = Q0\nJ2 = Q1Q0, K2 = Q0\nStep 4: circuit: three J-K flip-flops on one clock with two AND gates (Q2′Q0 and Q1Q0).\nStep 5: lock-out check. From 110: Q0 toggles → 1; J1 = 0, K1 = Q0 = 0 → Q1 stays 1; J2 = 0, K2 = 0 → Q2 stays 1 → 111. From 111: Q0 → 0, K1 = 1 → Q1 → 0, J2 = K2 = 1 → Q2 → 0 → 000. So both unused states return to the main sequence: no lock-out."],
    [7, "Design a synchronous BCD (decade) counter using J-K flip-flops.", "Step 1: 10 states need 4 flip-flops; the sequence is 0000, 0001 … 1001, 0000. States 1010–1111 are don’t-cares.\nStep 2: excitation table: build the J and K columns for each present state.\nStep 3: K-maps (with X in 10–15) give\nJ0 = K0 = 1\nJ1 = Q3′Q0, K1 = Q0\nJ2 = K2 = Q1Q0\nJ3 = Q2Q1Q0, K3 = Q0\nStep 4: circuit: four J-K flip-flops on a common clock and AND gates for Q3′Q0, Q1Q0 and Q2Q1Q0.\nStep 5: check 9 → 0: at 1001, J1 = Q3′Q0 = 0 and K1 = 1 → Q1 stays 0; J2 = K2 = Q1Q0 = 0 → Q2 stays 0; J3 = 0 and K3 = Q0 = 1 → Q3 resets to 0; Q0 toggles to 0 → 0000 ✓."],
    [7, "Explain ring and Johnson counters with circuits, state tables and timing diagrams.", "Ring counter: four D flip-flops, Q3 → D0. It must be preset to 1000. States (Q0Q1Q2Q3): 1000, 0100, 0010, 0001, 1000. N flip-flops give N states; each state has one output HIGH, so no decoder is needed. A single 1 moves one place per clock. If it starts at 0000 it stays there.\nJohnson (twisted-ring) counter: Q3′ → D0, starts at 0000. States: 0000, 1000, 1100, 1110, 1111, 0111, 0011, 0001. N flip-flops give 2N states. Each state is decoded by one 2-input AND gate (for example 0000 = Q0′Q3′ and 1000 = Q0Q1′). Each output is a square wave shifted by one clock.\nComparison:\nFeedback: ring Q3 → D0; Johnson Q3′ → D0.\nStates with N flip-flops: ring N; Johnson 2N.\nStart state: ring 1000 (preset needed); Johnson 0000.\nDecoding: ring none (one-hot); Johnson one 2-input AND gate per state."],
    [7, "Explain the four types of shift registers (SISO, SIPO, PISO, PIPO) with neat diagrams and data-movement examples.", "A register stores a binary word; a shift register also moves the bits one place per clock. The names show how data enters and leaves.\nSISO (serial-in serial-out): D flip-flops in a chain, each Q feeds the next D. One bit enters per clock and appears at the output N clocks later (a delay line). Example: input 1, 1, 0, 1 into 4 bits gives 1000, 1100, 0110, 1011 after the four clocks.\nSIPO: the same chain with every Q brought out; after N clocks the serial word is available in parallel (serial-to-parallel conversion).\nPISO: a 2:1 multiplexer in front of every flip-flop selects LOAD (parallel data) or SHIFT (previous stage). Load the word with LOAD = 1, then shift it out serially with LOAD = 0 (parallel-to-serial conversion).\nPIPO: every flip-flop has its own data input and output; all bits load on one clock. It is a storage register without shifting.\nSerial transfer of an n-bit word takes n clocks."],
    [7, "Design a 2-bit synchronous up/down counter with control input X (0 = up, 1 = down) and output Z = 1 in state 11. Draw the state diagram and state table and find the equations.", "Step 1: states 00, 01, 10, 11. X = 0: 00 → 01 → 10 → 11 → 00. X = 1: 00 → 11 → 10 → 01 → 00. Z = 1 in state 11 (Moore output).\nStep 2: state table (Q1Q0, X → next state, Z):\n00,0 → 01; 00,1 → 11; 01,0 → 10; 01,1 → 00; 10,0 → 11; 10,1 → 01; 11,0 → 00; 11,1 → 10. Z = 0 in 00, 01, 10 and Z = 1 in 11.\nStep 3: with D flip-flops: D0 = Q0′ (Q0 toggles every clock); D1 = X ⊕ Q1 ⊕ Q0; Z = Q1Q0.\nCheck: Q = 01, X = 0: D1 = 0 ⊕ 0 ⊕ 1 = 1, D0 = 0 → 10 ✓. Q = 00, X = 1: D1 = 1 ⊕ 0 ⊕ 0 = 1, D0 = 1 → 11 ✓.\nStep 4: circuit: two D flip-flops, an inverter for D0, two XOR gates for D1 and an AND gate for Z."],
  ],
  [
    [3, "What is a decoder? Draw a 2-to-4 decoder with enable and write its truth table.", "A decoder converts an n-bit code into 2ⁿ outputs, only one of which is active at a time. Each output is one minterm.\n2-to-4 decoder with enable EN: D0 = A1′A0′·EN, D1 = A1′A0·EN, D2 = A1A0′·EN, D3 = A1A0·EN.\nTruth table: EN = 0 → all outputs 0. EN = 1: A1A0 = 00 → D0 = 1; 01 → D1 = 1; 10 → D2 = 1; 11 → D3 = 1.\nCircuit: two inverters and four 3-input AND gates. Each AND gate detects one input combination; EN = 0 turns all outputs off."],
    [3, "Implement a full adder using a 3-to-8 decoder.", "The decoder gives every minterm of A, B, Cin (A = MSB). An OR gate adds the minterms of each output.\nS = Σm(1, 2, 4, 7) = D1 + D2 + D4 + D7\nCout = Σm(3, 5, 6, 7) = D3 + D5 + D6 + D7\nCircuit: 3-to-8 decoder with inputs A, B, Cin and two 4-input OR gates (or four-input NAND gates with active-LOW decoder outputs)."],
    [3, "Construct a 4-to-16 decoder using two 3-to-8 decoders.", "A2 A1 A0 go to both 3-to-8 decoders. The enable inputs are driven by A3: A3 = 0 enables decoder #1 (outputs Y0–Y7), through an inverter; A3 = 1 enables decoder #2 (outputs Y8–Y15). Exactly one decoder is active at a time, so exactly one of the 16 outputs is on."],
    [3, "Explain a seven-segment display and the BCD-to-7-segment decoder.", "A digit is formed by seven LED segments labelled a to g. The decoder turns a 4-bit BCD code into the seven segment signals.\nCommon-cathode displays need active-HIGH outputs (decoder IC 7448); common-anode displays need active-LOW outputs (IC 7447).\nBCD inputs 1010–1111 never occur, so they are don’t-cares. Example: BCD 0101 lights a, c, d, f, g to show 5."],
    [3, "Design a 4:1 multiplexer.", "A multiplexer has 4 data inputs I0–I3, 2 select lines S1 S0 and one output Y.\nY = S1′S0′·I0 + S1′S0·I1 + S1S0′·I2 + S1S0·I3.\nCircuit: two inverters, four 3-input AND gates and one 4-input OR gate. Each AND gate passes its input when its select code appears. S1S0 = 10 connects I2 to the output, like a rotary switch."],
    [3, "Implement F(A, B, C) = Σm(1, 3, 5, 6) using an 8:1 multiplexer.", "Connect A, B, C to the select inputs S2, S1, S0. The data input Iᵢ is the value of F in row i of the truth table.\nI1 = I3 = I5 = I6 = 1 and I0 = I2 = I4 = I7 = 0.\nSo connect I1, I3, I5, I6 to logic 1 and the others to logic 0. The output Y equals F."],
    [3, "Construct an 8:1 multiplexer using 4:1 and 2:1 multiplexers.", "Two 4:1 multiplexers, both with select lines S1 S0, choose one of I0–I3 and one of I4–I7. A 2:1 multiplexer controlled by S2 picks between their outputs: S2 = 0 selects I0–I3, S2 = 1 selects I4–I7. Total: two 4:1 and one 2:1 multiplexer."],
    [3, "Design a 1:4 demultiplexer. How is it related to a decoder?", "One data input D is sent to one of four outputs Y0–Y3, chosen by S1 S0; the other outputs are 0.\nYᵢ = D·mᵢ(S1, S0): Y0 = D·S1′S0′, Y1 = D·S1′S0, Y2 = D·S1S0′, Y3 = D·S1S0.\nThe circuit is identical to a 2-to-4 decoder whose enable input is used as the data input D. So a decoder with enable is a demultiplexer."],
    [3, "Construct a 1:8 demultiplexer using 1:2 and 1:4 demultiplexers.", "A 1:2 demultiplexer controlled by S2 routes D to the upper or lower 1:4 demultiplexer. Each 1:4 demultiplexer, with select lines S1 S0, sends D to one of its four outputs. Outputs Y0–Y3 are used when S2 = 0 and Y4–Y7 when S2 = 1. Total: one 1:2 and two 1:4 demultiplexers."],
    [3, "Design an 8:3 (octal-to-binary) encoder and state its limitation.", "Inputs D0–D7 (one active), outputs Y2 Y1 Y0:\nY2 = D4 + D5 + D6 + D7\nY1 = D2 + D3 + D6 + D7\nY0 = D1 + D3 + D5 + D7\nThree 4-input OR gates; D0 is not connected.\nLimitations: (1) if two inputs are active together the output code is wrong; (2) output 000 cannot tell D0 from “no input active”."],
    [3, "Design a 4:2 priority encoder.", "The highest-numbered active input wins. With K-maps:\nY1 = D3 + D2\nY0 = D3 + D2′D1\nV (valid) = D3 + D2 + D1 + D0\nV = 0 means no input is active, which removes the 000 ambiguity of the plain encoder."],
    [3, "Compare PROM, PLA and PAL.", "PROM: AND array fixed (a full decoder with all 2ⁿ minterms), OR array programmable; outputs are written directly as Σm; simple, but size doubles with each input.\nPAL: AND array programmable, OR array fixed; cheaper and faster, but each output has a fixed number of product terms.\nPLA: both arrays programmable; product terms can be shared between outputs; most flexible but slower and costlier.\nNotation: × = programmed (fuse intact) connection; • = fixed connection."],
    [7, "Design a 3-to-8 line decoder. Using it, implement a full adder.", "Step 1: 3 inputs A2 A1 A0 give 8 outputs D0–D7; exactly one output is 1, the one whose number equals the input code.\nStep 2: truth table: input 000 → D0 = 1, 001 → D1 = 1, … 111 → D7 = 1.\nStep 3: equations: D0 = A2′A1′A0′, D1 = A2′A1′A0, … D7 = A2A1A0.\nStep 4: circuit: three inverters and eight 3-input AND gates.\nStep 5: full adder: apply A, B, Cin to A2 A1 A0. S = Σm(1, 2, 4, 7), Cout = Σm(3, 5, 6, 7). OR the matching outputs.\nStep 6: check A = 1, B = 1, Cin = 0 → D6 is active → the S gate (D1, D2, D4, D7) gives 0 and the Cout gate (D3, D5, D6, D7) gives 1 → 1 + 1 + 0 = 10₂ ✓.\nFinal: S = D1 + D2 + D4 + D7, Cout = D3 + D5 + D6 + D7."],
    [7, "Design a BCD-to-seven-segment decoder for a common-cathode display. Show the truth table, K-maps and equations.", "Step 1: common cathode: a segment is ON when its input is 1. Inputs ABCD (A = MSB); segments a to g.\nStep 2: BCD inputs 1010–1111 never occur, so they are don’t-cares (X).\nStep 3: truth table (segments ON): 0: abcdef; 1: bc; 2: abdeg; 3: abcdg; 4: bcfg; 5: acdfg; 6: acdefg; 7: abc; 8: abcdefg; 9: abcdfg.\nStep 4: K-map simplification with don’t-cares gives:\na = A + C + BD + B′D′\nb = B′ + CD + C′D′\nc = B + C′ + D\nd = A + B′D′ + B′C + CD′ + BC′D\ne = B′D′ + CD′\nf = A + C′D′ + BC′ + BD′\ng = A + BC′ + B′C + CD′\nStep 5: circuit: inverters for A–D and AND–OR networks for each segment (or a ROM). IC 7448 does this.\nCheck BCD 2 (0010): a = 0 + 1 = 1; b = 1; c = 0 + 0 + 0 = 0 ✓ (segment c is off for 2); e = 0 + 1 = 1 ✓."],
    [7, "Design a 4:1 multiplexer. Implement F(A, B, C) = Σm(1, 3, 5, 6) with a 4:1 MUX and F(A, B, C, D) = Σm(1, 3, 4, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15) with an 8:1 MUX.", "4:1 MUX: Y = S1′S0′I0 + S1′S0I1 + S1S0′I2 + S1S0I3 (four AND gates, one OR gate, two inverters).\nF(A, B, C) = Σm(1, 3, 5, 6) with a 4:1 MUX: use A, B as select inputs S1 S0 and C as the data. For AB = 00: minterms m0, m1 → F = C. AB = 01: m2, m3 → F = C. AB = 10: m4, m5 → F = C. AB = 11: m6 = 1, m7 = 0 → F = C′.\nSo I0 = I1 = I2 = C and I3 = C′.\nF(A, B, C, D) with an 8:1 MUX: select inputs S2 S1 S0 = A B C, data from D. ABC = 000: m0, m1 → F = D; 001: m2, m3 → D; 010: m4 = 1, m5 = 0 → D′; 011: m6, m7 = 0 → 0; 100: m8, m9 = 0 → 0; 101: m10 = 0, m11 = 1 → D; 110: m12, m13 = 1 → 1; 111: m14, m15 = 1 → 1.\nSo I0 = D, I1 = D, I2 = D′, I3 = 0, I4 = 0, I5 = D, I6 = 1, I7 = 1."],
    [7, "Explain 1:4 and 1:8 demultiplexers with logic diagrams. Construct a 1:8 demultiplexer tree and explain the use of a decoder as a demultiplexer.", "1:4 demultiplexer: data D goes to the output chosen by S1 S0. Y0 = D·S1′S0′, Y1 = D·S1′S0, Y2 = D·S1S0′, Y3 = D·S1S0 (two inverters, four 3-input AND gates).\n1:8 demultiplexer: Yᵢ = D·mᵢ(S2, S1, S0): eight 4-input AND gates with three select lines.\nTree: a 1:2 demultiplexer driven by S2 feeds two 1:4 demultiplexers, both with S1 S0. S2 = 0 sends D to Y0–Y3 and S2 = 1 to Y4–Y7.\nDecoder as demultiplexer: a decoder with enable has Yᵢ = EN·mᵢ. If the enable is used as the data input D and the decoder inputs as the select lines, the circuit is a demultiplexer. A 3-to-8 decoder with enable is a 1:8 demultiplexer."],
    [7, "Design an 8:3 encoder and explain its drawbacks. Design a 4:2 priority encoder.", "8:3 encoder: Y2 = D4 + D5 + D6 + D7; Y1 = D2 + D3 + D6 + D7; Y0 = D1 + D3 + D5 + D7 (three 4-input OR gates).\nDrawbacks: two or more active inputs give a wrong code (for example D3 and D5 together give 111); and 000 is the output both for D0 and for no input.\n4:2 priority encoder: the highest-numbered active input wins. Truth table (D3 D2 D1 D0 → Y1 Y0 V): 0000 → XX 0; 0001 → 00 1; 001X → 01 1; 01XX → 10 1; 1XXX → 11 1.\nK-maps: Y1 = D3 + D2; Y0 = D3 + D2′D1; V = D3 + D2 + D1 + D0.\nV = 0 shows that no input is active."],
    [7, "Implement a full adder using a PROM, and F1 = AB + AC, F2 = AC + BC using a PLA and a PAL. Compare the three devices.", "PROM (8 × 2): inputs A, B, Cin, a fixed AND array giving all eight minterms and a programmable OR array: S = Σm(1, 2, 4, 7), Cout = Σm(3, 5, 6, 7). Mark × at those minterm–output crossings.\nPLA: both arrays are programmable. The functions F1 = AB + AC and F2 = AC + BC need the product terms AB, AC and BC. AC is shared by both outputs, so only 3 AND lines are used. The OR array connects AB and AC to F1 and AC and BC to F2.\nPAL: the OR array is fixed, so each output has its own AND terms: F1 gets AB and AC; F2 gets AC and BC. AC is generated twice, so 4 AND lines are used.\nComparison: PROM: fixed AND, programmable OR, no minimisation, grows 2ⁿ. PLA: both programmable, shares product terms, most flexible, costlier and slower. PAL: programmable AND, fixed OR, cheapest and fastest, no sharing."],
  ],
  [
    [3, "Explain memory organisation. How many address and data lines does a 16K × 8 memory need?", "A memory is a set of locations (words); each has a unique address and holds m bits. A 2ⁿ × m memory has 2ⁿ words of m bits, needs n address lines (decoded to one of 2ⁿ word lines) and m data lines.\n16K = 16 × 1024 = 2¹⁴, so 14 address lines and 8 data lines. Capacity = 16K × 8 = 128 Kbit.\nControl signals: CS (chip select), WE (write enable) and OE (output enable)."],
    [3, "Explain the 6-transistor SRAM cell.", "Two cross-coupled inverters (4 transistors) form a latch holding Q and Q′. Two access transistors, turned on by the word line WL, connect the latch to the bit lines BL and BL′.\nWL = 0: hold (no refresh needed). WL = 1: read (sense the bit lines) or write (drive BL and BL′ with strong drivers to flip the latch)."],
    [3, "Explain the DRAM cell and the need for refresh.", "One transistor and one capacitor (1T1C): a charged capacitor stores 1 and a discharged one stores 0. The cell is very small, so DRAM has high density.\nThe charge leaks away in milliseconds, and below a threshold a 1 would read as 0, so every row must be read and rewritten about every 64 ms. A read is destructive, so the sense amplifier restores the value."],
    [3, "Compare SRAM and DRAM.", "SRAM: 6-transistor latch cell; fast; no refresh; larger area and higher cost per bit; used for cache.\nDRAM: 1 transistor + 1 capacitor; slower; needs refresh and restore after a read; very dense and cheap; address multiplexed with RAS and CAS; used for main memory."],
    [3, "Draw and explain the SRAM read-cycle timing.", "The address is applied; then CS and OE go LOW. After the access time tAA the data output leaves high-impedance (high-Z) and becomes valid. The next read can begin after the read-cycle time tRC (the minimum time between successive reads). When CS or OE goes HIGH the data pins return to high-Z."],
    [3, "What is address multiplexing in DRAM?", "The address is sent in two halves on the same pins. The row address is placed on the pins and latched by the falling edge of RAS; then the column address is placed on the same pins and latched by the falling edge of CAS. This halves the number of address pins (for example 10 instead of 20 for 1M locations)."],
    [3, "How many 1K × 4 chips are needed to build a 4K × 8 memory? How are they connected?", "Number of chips = (4K × 8) / (1K × 4) = 8 chips, arranged as 4 rows × 2 chips.\nWord-size expansion: in each row two chips side by side give 8 bits (D0–D3 and D4–D7).\nCapacity expansion: A0–A9 go to all chips; A11 A10 go to a 2-to-4 decoder whose four outputs select one row through CS."],
    [3, "Explain the architecture of a CPLD.", "Several PAL-like function blocks (AND–OR array and macrocells) are joined by one programmable interconnect matrix (PIM); I/O blocks connect to the pins. A macrocell contains a flip-flop and output logic.\nIt is non-volatile (EEPROM/Flash), works as soon as power is applied, and every path goes through the PIM once, so the timing is predictable."],
    [3, "Explain the architecture of an FPGA.", "A two-dimensional array of configurable logic blocks (CLBs, each with LUTs and flip-flops) separated by routing channels with programmable switch boxes, and surrounded by I/O blocks. Modern devices also have block RAM, DSP multipliers and clock managers. The configuration is stored in SRAM bits loaded at power-up."],
    [3, "How does a look-up table implement a logic function? Show for F = Σm(1, 2, 4, 7).", "A 3-input LUT is a small memory of 8 SRAM cells that stores the truth table, read out by an 8:1 multiplexer whose select lines are the inputs A, B, C.\nFor F = Σm(1, 2, 4, 7) (the sum of a full adder) the stored bits for inputs 000 to 111 are 0 1 1 0 1 0 0 1. The inputs choose the stored value. Any function of 3 variables fits; a flip-flop after the LUT gives registered output."],
    [3, "Draw a MOD-60 counter used in a digital clock.", "MOD-60 = MOD-10 (units, BCD counter 0–9) cascaded with MOD-6 (tens, 0–5). The units counter’s terminal count (9) enables the tens counter. At 59 both reset to 00 and a carry pulse is sent to the minutes counter (or the hours counter)."],
    [3, "Draw the state diagram of a four-state traffic light controller.", "S0 (NS green), S1 (NS yellow), S2 (EW green), S3 (EW yellow). TD = 1 (timer done) moves to the next state (S0 → S1 → S2 → S3 → S0); TD = 0 holds the state.\nGray codes 00, 01, 11, 10 change one bit per transition. A red light is on for the road that does not have green or yellow."],
    [7, "Explain the internal organisation of a static RAM with its cell, read and write timing diagrams. Show how a 2K × 8 memory is built from 1K × 8 chips.", "Step 1: organisation: a 2ⁿ × m memory has n address lines → decoder → one of 2ⁿ word lines, and m bidirectional data lines.\nStep 2: chip signals: address in, control in (CS, WE, OE), data in/out. When the chip is off, the data pins are high-Z.\nStep 3: the SRAM cell: a latch (two inverters, 4 transistors) with 2 access transistors; WL = 1 connects the cell to BL and BL′ for read or write.\nStep 4: read cycle: data is valid tAA after a valid address; the next read can start after tRC.\nStep 5: write cycle: data must be stable tDS before and tDH after WE goes HIGH, and WE must stay LOW for at least tWP.\nStep 6: 2K × 8 from 1K × 8 (capacity expansion): two chips share A0–A9 and D0–D7. A10 = 0 selects chip #1 (addresses 0–1023) and A10 = 1 selects chip #2 (1024–2047); A10 and its complement drive the CS inputs.\nFinal: SRAM = an array of 6-T latch cells + address decoder + read/write circuits, controlled by CS, WE and OE with the timing parameters tRC, tAA, tWC, tDS and tDH."],
    [7, "Explain the DRAM cell, its read/write operation, refresh and address multiplexing with timing. Compare DRAM with SRAM.", "Step 1: cell: one transistor + one capacitor; charged = 1, discharged = 0. Very small, so high density.\nStep 2: write: WL = 1 and the bit line charges or discharges the capacitor. Read: WL = 1; charge sharing gives a few mV on the bit line; the sense amplifier amplifies it and writes the value back (the read is destructive).\nStep 3: refresh: leakage removes the charge in milliseconds, so a refresh controller reads and rewrites every row within about 64 ms (RAS-only or CAS-before-RAS refresh).\nStep 4: address multiplexing: the row address is latched at the falling edge of RAS and the column address at the falling edge of CAS, so an N-bit address needs only N/2 pins.\nStep 5: organisation: cells are in a square array; the row decoder selects a word line, all sense amplifiers of that row are activated, and the column decoder picks the required bits.\nStep 6: comparison: SRAM is faster, needs no refresh, has a 6-transistor cell and is used for cache; DRAM is denser and cheaper, needs refresh, is slower and is used for main memory.\nFinal: DRAM stores bits as capacitor charge; reads are destructive and need restore, the cells need periodic refresh, and the row and column addresses are multiplexed with RAS and CAS."],
    [7, "Explain the architectures of CPLD and FPGA with neat diagrams. Show how a LUT implements a logic function and compare the two devices.", "Step 1: one programmable chip can implement a whole design and be changed by reprogramming.\nStep 2: CPLD: several PAL-like function blocks (AND–OR array + macrocells, a macrocell being a flip-flop + output logic) linked by a programmable interconnect matrix (PIM), with I/O blocks. Non-volatile, instant-on, predictable delay because each path crosses the PIM once.\nStep 3: FPGA: configurable logic blocks (LUT + flip-flop) in an array, programmable routing channels with switch boxes, I/O blocks, block RAM, DSP blocks and clock managers.\nStep 4: LUT: a 3-input LUT stores the truth table in SRAM cells, selected by the inputs through a multiplexer. For F = Σm(1, 2, 4, 7) it stores 0 1 1 0 1 0 0 1. An optional flip-flop and output multiplexer give registered or combinational output.\nStep 5: comparison:\nCPLD: a few large blocks; EEPROM/Flash; non-volatile; predictable timing; hundreds of macrocells; small designs.\nFPGA: many small LUT-based blocks; SRAM configuration (needs loading at power-up); routing delay depends on placement; very large (millions of gates).\nFinal: CPLD = few large PAL-like blocks + central interconnect, non-volatile, predictable. FPGA = many small LUT-based CLBs + distributed routing, SRAM-configured, very large."],
    [7, "Design a digital clock that displays hours, minutes and seconds. Draw the block diagram and explain each counter.", "Step 1: display HH:MM:SS with hours 01–12 and minutes and seconds 00–59 from a 1 Hz time base.\nStep 2: block diagram: a chain of frequency dividers and counters; the 1 Hz clock enters the seconds-units counter and each counter’s carry enables the next.\nStep 3: time base: 60 Hz mains → MOD-60 counter → 1 Hz; or a 32.768 kHz crystal → 15 cascaded T flip-flops → 32768 / 2¹⁵ = 1 Hz.\nStep 4: seconds counter: MOD-10 units (BCD counter 0–9) cascaded with MOD-6 tens (0–5), the tens enabled by the units terminal count (9). At 59 → 00 the tens counter is cleared (detect 6 = 110 with NAND(Q2, Q1), or a synchronous reset) and one pulse goes to the minutes counter. The minutes counter is identical and is enabled once per minute.\nStep 5: hours counter: 01 → 12 → 01. Detect 13 (tens = 1 and units = 0011) and load 01. An AM/PM flip-flop toggles when the hour changes from 11 to 12.\nStep 6: six BCD-to-7-segment decoders drive the displays. Set buttons inject pulses into the minutes and hours counters through 2:1 multiplexers.\nFinal: digital clock = 1 Hz divider + MOD-60 seconds + MOD-60 minutes + 12-hour counter with the 13 → 01 correction + 7-segment decoders."],
    [7, "Design a traffic light controller for a two-road intersection. Give the state diagram, state table, equations, circuit and timing diagram.", "Step 1: specification: NS green 30 s → NS yellow 5 s → EW green 30 s → EW yellow 5 s → repeat. Structure: a timer (datapath) + a 4-state FSM (control) + an output decoder. The timer gives TD (time done) at the end of each interval, and the state tells the timer which delay to load.\nStep 2: states: S0 = NS green (00), S1 = NS yellow (01), S2 = EW green (11), S3 = EW yellow (10), in Gray code.\nStep 3: state table (present state, TD → next state): TD = 0 → hold; TD = 1: 00 → 01, 01 → 11, 11 → 10, 10 → 00. Outputs (Moore): S0: NS green, EW red; S1: NS yellow, EW red; S2: NS red, EW green; S3: NS red, EW yellow.\nStep 4: with TD = 1: Q1⁺ = Q0, Q0⁺ = Q1′ (check 00 → 01 → 11 → 10 → 00). With TD = 0 the state holds.\nStep 5: equations with D flip-flops: D1 = TD′·Q1 + TD·Q0, D0 = TD′·Q0 + TD·Q1′. Each is a 2:1 multiplexer selected by TD. The lamps are decoded from Q1Q0.\nStep 6: check: from S1 (01) with TD = 1: D1 = Q0 = 1, D0 = Q1′ = 1 → 11 = S2, so EW turns green. Exactly one light per road is on.\nFinal: 2 D flip-flops with D1 = TD′Q1 + TD·Q0 and D0 = TD′Q0 + TD·Q1′; lamps decoded from Q1Q0; the timer loads 30 s or 5 s (selected by Q1 ⊕ Q0)."],
    [7, "Design a circuit that displays the mobile number 9848502731 one digit per clock pulse on a seven-segment display.", "Step 1: display 9 8 4 8 5 0 2 7 3 1 one digit per clock pulse and repeat. The digit 8 appears twice, so the digit itself cannot be the counter state. Use a MOD-10 counter for the position (state 0–9) and a combinational converter for the digit.\nStep 2: converter truth table (position Q3Q2Q1Q0 → digit N3N2N1N0): 0000 → 1001 (9); 0001 → 1000 (8); 0010 → 0100 (4); 0011 → 1000 (8); 0100 → 0101 (5); 0101 → 0000 (0); 0110 → 0010 (2); 0111 → 0111 (7); 1000 → 0011 (3); 1001 → 0001 (1). Positions 1010–1111 are don’t-cares.\nStep 3: K-maps with the don’t-cares:\nN3 = Q3′Q2′(Q0 + Q1′)\nN2 = Q2Q1Q0 + Q2Q1′Q0′ + Q2′Q1Q0′\nN1 = Q2Q1 + Q3Q0′\nN0 = Q3 + Q2Q1Q0 + Q1′Q0′\nStep 4: check position 3 (0011): N3 = 1·1·(1 + 0) = 1; N2 = 0; N1 = 0; N0 = 0 → 1000 = 8 ✓. Position 7 (0111): N3 = 0; N2 = 1; N1 = 1; N0 = 1 → 0111 = 7 ✓.\nStep 5: circuit: MOD-10 counter (for example 74160 or 7490) → the four converter gates → BCD-to-7-segment decoder 7448 → common-cathode display. Alternative: store the digits in a 16 × 4 PROM addressed by the counter.\nFinal: sequence generator = MOD-10 counter + code converter (N3–N0 above) + BCD-to-7-segment decoder."],
  ],
];
